 coverage in `anatomy-map.json` improved from 24/27 to **25/27**: `Tibialis anterior` was a false gap — `FJ1439`/`FJ1439M` are tagged `skeletal` in the atlas although they are muscle meshes, so the filter dropped them. The two remaining gaps (**Latissimus dorsi**, **Rectus abdominis**) are genuine absences in BodyParts3D and stay declared as gaps rather than approximated.\n\n## The blocker: the atlas is not an articulated skeleton\n\nThis is not a bug I can fix by fitting better. Measured from the meshes themselves:\n\n- left femur `FJ3259` spans y **0.446–0.912 m**\n- left hip bone `FJ3288` spans y **0.830–1.036 m**\n- sacrum spans y **0.861–1.005 m**\n\nThe femoral head sits ~35 cm *below* the acetabulum. The left knee fits at y≈0.479 while the femoral head fits at y≈0.472 — `meanError` 50 mm on both hips versus ~5 mm everywhere else tells the same story. BodyParts3D ships **shape**, one part per concept, in a shared display frame; it never ships **assembly**.\n\nSo every cross-bone joint centre is geometrically self-consistent per bone and anatomically contradictory as a limb. A rig built on these numbers would animate a body whose segments don't connect.\n\n## The product decision I need from you\n\nThere are three honest paths, and I won't pick one silently because they trade off differently for real users:\n\n1. **Authored proportions, real shapes** — keep BodyParts3D for what it is good at (believable bone geometry, per-bone shape and landmarks) and author the *skeleton*: segment ratios anchored to the 1.714 m stature, hip/knee/ankle/shoulder/elbow/wrist positions from standard anthropometry, each bone scaled and seated on its authored parent joint. Every measurement is then correct-by-construction, and the diagnostic record states plainly that the assembly is authored over the model's shape rather than fitted from it. Cost: the joint positions are no longer \"measured\" — they are documented adult-anthropometric ratios.\n\n2. **Fit per chain, not per joint** — solve each limb as a kinematic chain against the bone silhouettes so the segments connect by least squares rather than by ratio. It recovers some person-specific proportion signal, but the femur/hip offset above means the chain would be stretched well beyond anatomical plausibility, so I'd be fitting to a defect. I don't recommend this.\n\n3. **Replace the anatomy for the rig** — keep BodyParts3D for the study/atlas view (where it's excellent) and use a properly articulated open model for the animated practice rig. This adds a vendored dependency with its own licence to carry, but it's the only option where fitted joints are *true* rather than authored.\n\nMy recommendation is **1 now, 3 as a follow-up**: authored proportions get a real, operable practice product shipping, with the limitation stated in the UI and the diagnostics; option 3 is then a swap behind the same rig interface if you want fitted joints later.\n\n**What I need from you:** a one-word choice — `authored`, `chain`, or `replace` — and whether the follow-up swap to an articulated model is wanted. On that answer I'll build out `portal/movement/` (catalog + zh/en resolver, safety triage, plan engine, session player with voice cues and telemetry, the rig renderer and pose library, muscle role per phase, capability profile, attribution), wire it into the portal, add Playwright verification against `http://127.0.0.1:4173`, and land it on a working branch with a truthful execution report.\n\nEverything so far is uncommitted and confined to new files under `portal/assets/movement/`, `portal/data/movement/`, and `scripts/build-*.mjs` — no existing Movement page, no workflow, no credential has been touched."}],"stop_reason":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":43499,"output_tokens":1182,"cache_read_input_tokens":43008,"total_tokens":44681}}},{"type":"result","subtype":"success","uuid":"a8d1c67f-0645-46de-a0d4-75d15cb89f8c","session_id":"cef6745e-92bb-4fe4-9624-333962f2ed08","is_error":false,"duration_ms":769838,"